": "Los Angeles, United States", "role": "Cultural Influence"}, {"name": "Chicago, United States", "role": "Publication Site"}, {"name": "New York City, United States", "role": "Literary Hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Fahrenheit 451", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "fahrenheit-451"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q202009", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fahrenheit_451"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Fahrenheit 451 contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Fahrenheit 451 has significantly influenced the discourse on censorship and the importance of literature, becoming a foundational text in understanding the risks of totalitarianism and mass media influence.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-10T21:24:00Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27307158361", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1953 novel by ray bradbury Created by Ray Bradbury. Created c. 1951.", "newValue": "Fahrenheit 451, published in 1953, is a dystopian novel by Ray Bradbury that serves as a profound commentary on the perils of censorship and the suppression of dissenting ideas. Set in an imagined future where books are banned and 'firemen' burn any that are found, the narrative explores themes of conformity, technology’s role in society, and the conflict between knowledge and ignorance. The novel not only reflects the anxieties of the Cold War era but also resonates with contemporary global concerns about freedom of expression. It has become a critical text in discussions about the value of literature in a democratic society and the dangers of authoritarianism.

The plot follows Guy Montag, a fireman who begins to question the oppressive regime in which he lives. Through Montag's transformation, Bradbury critiques the loss of critical thought and the impact of mass media on human relationships and perception. The novel was conceived during a period of intense political scrutiny in the United States, particularly regarding McCarthyism and the Red Scare, which directly influenced its themes. Montag's eventual rebellion against the state symbolizes the struggle for intellectual freedom and the importance of preserving cultural heritage, making it a pivotal work in American literature.

